comments.body:
@participant3 I agree, directionally. In fact, I think the "AI Usage Policy" should explicitly state the goals, why it exist, before going into the actual policy.

You're spot on that the biggest problem with AI usage is that the cost to the contributor of producing a PR dropped to almost zero and instead the cost is shifted to maintainers.

The main question is what mechanisms can we introduce in the policy that are actually _effective_. For instance, one of the most annoying things to me as a maintainer is when people submit a PR for which the CI does not run automatically (GH behavior), so I have to go and manually click Run CI button, only to find out that the basic linter is failing. We used checkboxes in the PR template but they were not very effective, especially because they are a one-time action when the PR is being created (and at that time perhaps the author did run the linters, but later changes break them).
